'''Frank Corsentino''' {{born|13|July|1941|died|7|January|2007}} was an actor who had three ''[[Star Trek]]'' appearances to his credit, playing three different [[Ferengi]] characters. Among those characters was [[Bok]] in the {{s|TNG}} [[TNG Season 1|first season]] episode {{e|The Battle}}. (Bok returned in the [[TNG Season 7|seventh season]] episode {{e|Bloodlines}}, but was played by [[Lee Arenberg]].) His costume from the latter appearance was sold off on the [[It's A Wrap! sale and auction]] on eBay and was previously worn by [[Rob Steiner]] and [[Lou Wagner]]. {{stala|5328}} His face as Bok was later used in the {{y|1995}} {{video game}} ''[[Star Trek: The Next Generation - A Final Unity]]'' as the Ferengi character Aramut. He went uncredited in the game, however.

[[File:Aramut.jpg|thumb|Corsentino's face as Bok, used for the character Aramut in ''A Final Unity'']]
Corsentino was [[Star Trek birthdays#July|born]] in San Pedro, California. In the 1970s, Corsentino guest-starred in two episodes of ''Gunsmoke'' with [[Glenn Corbett]], with both Corsentino and Corbett playing a different character each time. The first episode, 1971's "Phoenix", also guest-starred [[Mariette Hartley]], while the second episode, 1974's "A Family of Killers", featured [[Anthony Caruso]].

Corsentino also appeared on such shows as {{wt|Then Came Bronson}} (1970, with [[Skip Homeier]]), {{wt|Ironside (1967 TV series)| Ironside}} (1971, starring [[Barbara Anderson]], with [[Joel Grey]] and [[Gene Lyons]]), {{wt|The Courtship of Eddie's Father}} (with [[Alan Oppenheimer]]), {{wt|The Odd Couple (1970 TV series)|The Odd Couple}} (starring [[Garry Walberg]]), {{wt|Starsky & Hutch}} (1976, starring [[David Soul]]), {{wt|The Fantastic Journey}} (1977, written by [[Harold Livingston]]), {{wt|Vegas (1978 TV series)|Vegas}} (including an episode with [[Ian Abercrombie]] and [[Gary Lockwood]]), and {{wt|Hunter (1984 American TV series)|Hunter}} (1985, with [[Michael Dorn]]). His feature film credits include {{wt|Beyond the Valley of the Dolls}} (1970, with [[Charles Napier]]), {{wt|Simon, King of the Witches}} (1971, with [[Andrew Prine]]), ''Up Your Alley'' (with [[Eli Behar]] and [[Anthony Giger]]), and {{wt|Moonchild (1974 film)|Moonchild}} (1974).

Acting on television since the 1960s, Corsentino's role as [[Gegis]] in the {{s|VOY}} episode {{e|Inside Man}} was his last known TV appearance. Corsentino [[Star Trek deaths#January|passed away]] in 2007 in Hawthorne, California. {{el|legacy.com/us/obituaries/dailybreeze/name/frank-corsentino-obituary?id{{=}}25271237}}

