'''Danny Goldring''' {{born|31|May|1946|died|2|December|2022}} was a stage, television and film actor who made guest appearances in three of the ''[[Star Trek]]'' spin-off series. He appeared in a total of six episodes, playing five different characters. His costumes from the episodes {{e|Fortunate Son}} and {{e|The Catwalk}} were later sold off on the [[It's A Wrap! sale and auction]] on eBay. {{stala|3280}} {{stala|6484}}

Goldring was born in Woodstock, Illinois. As the son of a US Navy officer he traveled the world until he settled down in Chicago, Illinois. After he toured with the puppeteer troupe Cole Marionettes, Goldring attended the Goodman Theatre School of Drama to focus on a career as an actor. {{el|articles.chicagotribune.com/2012-09-28/entertainment/ct-ae-0930-kogan-sidewalks-20120929_1_tom-kane-danny-goldring-fictional-mayor}}

Goldring started his screen career in the 1970s with appearances in {{wt|The Million Dollar Rip-Off}} (1976, with [[James Sloyan]] and directed by [[Alexander Singer]]) and guest roles in episodes of ''The Awakening Land'' (1978, with [[Bert Remsen]] and [[Timothy Carhart]]), ''Texas'' (1980), and ''Crime Story'' (1986, with [[Bill Smitrovich]], [[William O. Campbell]], [[Linda Fetters]] and [[Michael Krawic]]).

Further television work includes {{wt|Dallas (TV series)|Dallas}} (1989, with [[Leslie Bevis]] and [[Joshua Harris]]), {{wt|Matlock (1986 TV series)|Matlock}} (1989, with [[Brooke Bundy]], [[Michael Durrell]], [[David Froman]], [[Jason Wingreen]] and [[Mark Chaet]]), {{wt|Hunter (1984 American TV series)|Hunter}} (1990, with [[Charles Hallahan]], [[Tony Plana]], Brooke Bundy and [[Tina Lifford]]), ''ABC Afterschool Specials'' (1993), ''Missing Persons'' (1993, with [[Kavi Raz]] and [[James Otis]]), {{wt|Renegade (TV series)|Renegade}} (1994, starring [[Branscombe Richmond]] and with [[Judson Scott]] and [[Joseph Campanella]]), {{wt|VR.5}} (1995, with [[Louise Fletcher]], [[Erick Avari]] and [[Tom Towles]]), {{wt|NYPD Blue}} (1995, with [[Sharon Lawrence]], [[Gordon Clapp]], [[Michael Rothhaar]], [[Deborah Taylor]] and [[Herschel Sparber]]), {{wt|Wings (1990 TV series)|Wings}} (1995, starring [[Steven Weber]], with [[Andrew Robinson]] and [[Tucker Smallwood]]), {{wt|Space: Above and Beyond}} (1995, starring [[Lanei Chapman]] and Tucker Smallwood), {{wt|ER (TV series)|ER}} (1995, starring [[Eriq La Salle]], with [[Lily Mariye]], [[Tony Plana]] and [[Mike Genovese]]),{{wt|EZ Streets}} (1996, with [[Mike Starr]] and [[Nichole McAuley]]), ''Nothing Sacred'' (1998, with [[Mark Harelik]], [[Cindy Katz]] and [[James Harper]]), ''Turks'' (1999, with Cindy Katz and [[Ernest Perry, Jr.]]), {{wt|The Practice}} (1999, with [[Stephen Macht]], [[Ann Gillespie]] and [[Saul Rubinek]]), and {{wt|Early Edition}} (1998 & 2000).

Film work include {{wt|Vice Versa (1988 film)|Vice Versa}} (1988, with [[Charles Lucia]], [[Peggy Roeder]], [[Richard Kind]] and [[James Hong]]), {{wt|Above the Law (1988 film)|Above the Law}} (1988, with Thalmus Rasulala), {{wt|The Package (1989 film)|The Package}} (1989, with [[Joanna Cassidy]], [[Thalmus Rasulala]] and [[Dennis Cockrum]]), the television crime drama ''Johnny Ryan'' (1990, with [[Clancy Brown]] and [[Cameron Thor]]), {{wt|The Babe}} (1992, with [[James Cromwell]], [[Bernard Kates]], [[Michael McGrady]], [[Shannon Cochran]], [[Michael Papajohn]], [[Larry Cedar]], [[Ken Gildin]], [[Harry Hutchinson]], Michael Krawic, [[Scott Haven]], [[Kara Zediker]] and [[Nick Satriano]]), the television thriller ''Legacy of Lies'' (1992, starring [[Martin Landau]], with [[Joe Morton]]), the unsold television pilot ''Angel Street'' (1992, with [[Michael Cavanaugh]] and [[Mark Rolston]]), the television thriller ''In the Company of Darkness'' (1993, with Steven Weber and Peggy Roeder), {{wt|Excessive Force (film)|Excessive Force}} (1993, with [[Tony Todd]], [[Tony Epper]], [[Bobby Bass]], [[Tom Hodges]], [[Mario Roberts]] and [[Carl Ciarfalio]]), {{wt|The Fugitive (1993 film)|The Fugitive}} (1993, with [[Andreas Katsulas]], [[Daniel Roebuck]], [[Richard Riehle]], [[Pancho Demmings]] and [[Jim Wilkey]]), {{wt|Natural Born Killers}} (1994, with [[Richard Lineback]] and Carl Ciarfalio), {{wt|Virtuosity}} (1995, with Louise Fletcher, [[Michael Buchman Silver]], [[Rogan Wilde]] and [[Tracee Lee Cocco]]), {{wt|Chain Reaction (1996 film)|Chain Reaction}} (1996, with Joanna Cassidy and [[Tzi Ma]]), {{wt|Bean (film)|Bean}} (1997, with [[Harris Yulin]], [[Larry Drake]], [[Dakin Matthews]], [[Peter Capaldi]], [[April Grace]], [[David Doty]], [[Robert Curtis Brown]] and [[Annette Helde]]), {{wt|Always Outnumbered}} (1998, with [[Bill Cobbs]] and [[John Cothran, Jr.]]), {{wt|Goodbye Lover}} (1998, with [[John Neville]], Michael Krawic, [[John Prosky]], [[Rob LaBelle]] and [[Leslie Jordan]]), and {{wt|Children of the Corn V: Fields of Terror}} (1998, with [[Fred Williamson]], [[Kane Hodder]], [[Hiro Koda]] and [[Deborah Strang]]).

Goldring guest-starred in {{wt|The Invisible Man (2000 TV series)|The Invisible Man}} (2001, with [[Armin Shimerman]] and [[Gary Weeks]]) and {{wt|Six Feet Under (TV series)|Six Feet Under}} (2005, with [[James Cromwell]], Joanna Cassidy, [[Lisa Banes]], [[Rosalind Chao]] and [[Andy Milder]]), appeared in the short projects ''Janey Van Winkle'' (2002), ''A Perfect Manhattan'' (2009), and ''Lenny Morris vs. The Universe'' (2011), and worked on {{wt|Just Visiting (film)|Just Visiting}} (2001, with [[Malcolm McDowell]], [[Darryl Henriques]], [[Ric Sarabia]] and [[Kelsey Grammer]]), ''Betaville'' (2001), the drama ''The Earl'' (2007), and {{wt|Chicago Overcoat}} (2009, with Mike Starr and [[Usman Ally]]).

In 2008, Goldring played "Grumpy", one of Joker's goons in {{wt|The Dark Knight}}. He filmed his scenes for eight days in Chicago and wasn't seen without a mask in this movie. Goldring remarked that, "''You might recognize my voice.''" {{el|articles.chicagotribune.com/2008-07-18/features/0807161052_1_comedy-fests-film-festival-tickets}} The film also featured ''[[Star Trek]]'' alumni [[Keith Szarabajka]], [[Tommy "Tiny" Lister, Jr.]], [[Andrew Bicknell]], [[Jon Lee Brody]] and [[Tom McComas]].

Between 2011 and 2012, Goldring portrayed Ryan Kavanaugh in {{wt|Boss (TV series)|Boss}}, starring Kelsey Grammer and with recurring guest stars [[Francis Guinan]] and [[Nicole Forester]]. {{el|articles.chicagotribune.com/2012-09-28/entertainment/ct-ae-0930-kogan-sidewalks-20120929_1_tom-kane-danny-goldring-fictional-mayor}} Goldring also performed in various stage plays such as "King of Coons", "Under Milkwood", "One Flew Over the Cuckoo's Nest", and "Porgy and Bess". {{el|goodmantheatre.org/artists-archive/creative-partners/actors/danny-goldring}}

He later appeared in the thriller ''The Drunk'' (2014, co-produced by [[Chadwick Struck]]), {{wt|Dig Two Graves}} (2014, with Kara Zediker), three episodes of [[Netflix]]' {{wt|Sense8}} (2015 & 2017, with [[Naveen Andrews]] and [[Ness Bautista]] and created and executive produced by [[J. Michael Straczynski]]), the television comedy ''Cop Drama'' (2015), the comedy ''Fools'' (2016), the short thriller ''Cellar Door'' (2016), the short comedy ''When Jeff Tried to Save the World'' (2017, with Jim O'Heir), and an episode of {{wt|Joe Pera Talks with You}} (2018).

Goldring died on December 2, 2022, from kidney failure. {{el|chicagoreader.com/arts-culture/performing-arts-feature/remembering-danny-goldring/}}

