'''Doug Jones''' {{born|24|May|1960}} is the actor who played [[Saru]] in {{s|DIS}} and the {{s|ST}} episode {{e|The Brightest Star}}.

Jones has been acting since the mid-1980s but was not prolific until he was cast in {{wt|Night Angel}} (1990, with [[Sam Hennings]]). In 1992, Jones appeared in {{wt|Batman Returns}} joining [[Anthony De Longis]], [[Branscombe Richmond]], [[Diane Salinger]], and [[Vincent Schiavelli]]. The next project for Jones was 1993's direct to video {{wt|Magic Kid}}, with [[Joseph Campanella]]. Next that year was {{wt|Hocus Pocus (1993 film)|Hocus Pocus}}, with [[Michael McGrady]] and [[Charles Rocket]]. Jones received further notice in {{wt|Tank Girl (film)|Tank Girl}} (1995, starring [[Lori Petty]] and [[Malcolm McDowell]], with [[Reg E. Cathey]], [[Jeff Kober]], [[Ann Magnuson]], [[Clayton Landey]], and [[Frank Welker]]).

In 1998, Jones played the monstrous Mother Bug in {{wt|Bug Buster}}, featuring [[George Takei]] and [[James Doohan]].

Typically cast as creatures in science fiction and fantasy projects, Jones is best known for his collaborations with Mexican filmmaker Guillermo del Toro, who cast him as {{w|Abe Sapien}} in {{wt|Hellboy (2004 film)|Hellboy}} (2004) and {{wt|Hellboy II: The Golden Army}} (2008), both starring [[Ron Perlman]]. He also played the Faun and the Pale Man in {{wt|Pan's Labyrinth}} (2006), and the {{w|Silver Surfer}} in {{wt|Fantastic Four: Rise of the Silver Surfer}} (2007). He played two demonic Imps in {{wt|Doom (film)|Doom}} (2005, starring [[Karl Urban]] and [[The Rock]]).

On television, Jones appeared as various alien creatures in {{wt|The Outer Limits (1995 TV series)|The Outer Limits}} revival series (1995-2002), which was narrated by [[Kevin Conway]]. He co-starred with [[Robert Picardo]] in one of those appearances, and was cast by [[Mary Jo Slater]] in all episodes.

For his role as Saru, Jones was nominated three times for a [[Saturn Award]] for Best Supporting Actor on a Television Series, {{st.com|article/discovery-nominated-for-5-saturn-awards}} winning the second one in 2019 and the third one in 2021.

